    "message": "The reason Brave and other apps on your iPhone can't reach local services via `localhost` or your Linux IP comes down to two main things:\n\n1. **`localhost` is local to the device:** On your iPhone, `localhost` (127.0.0.1) always means the iPhone itself, not your Linux computer.\n2. **Firewall / Binding Restrictions:** Your Linux machine is running **firewalld** on the tethered network interface (`enp0s20f0u3c4i2`), and many local development servers (like Vite, React, Node, Python `http.server`, etc.) bind only to `127.0.0.1` by default, meaning they reject external incoming requests from your iPhone even if you use `172.20.10.2`.\n\n### How to fix it:\n* **Bind your app to `0.0.0.0` or `172.20.10.2`:** Instead of starting your local app on `localhost` or `127.0.0.1`, start it listening on all interfaces (`0.0.0.0`) so it accepts traffic coming over the USB/hotspot network.\n* **Access it properly on your iPhone:** Open Brave and type your Linux computer's tethered IP address explicitly with the port (e.g., `http://172.20.10.2:3000`).\n* **Allow the port through firewalld (if needed):** If your app runs on a custom port, you can temporarily allow it via `sudo firewall-cmd --add-port=<port>/tcp --permanent && sudo firewall-cmd --reload`."
  }
}

